  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThe current algorithm uses local optimization techniques (coordinate and block descent) but may struggle with local optima. \nThe two-interval method is chosen due to theoretical results suggesting that the optimum has a two-interval structure. \nNotably, the optimal solution for functions on a circle has a two-interval structure, and although our problem is on a line, we represent it by combining two periodic functions. \nThis approach balances the trade-off between coverage and minimization of overlap.\n\nThe central idea is to:\n- Represent the function using two disjoint intervals on the [0,2] domain, ensuring the total measure is 1.\n- Sample these intervals at regular points to form a valid density function h.\n- Optimize the positions and lengths of the intervals to minimize the worst-case overlap.\n\nThis method avoids the high dimensionality of general optimization and leverages geometric intuition. The key limitation is that:\n- It assumes the optimal is two-interval (not proven for linear domain)\n- Requires careful interval placement and length optimization\n- Might not break very tight records\n\nLimitations addressed:\n- Use randomized search to improve coverage of the interval parameter space\n- Use different interval configurations (equal vs. different lengths)\n- Iterative strategy to avoid local optimum\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\n- Representation: h defined by two disjoint intervals, each with specified length and position\n- Objective: minimize the maximum overlap integral C = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints:\n  - \u222b h(x)dx = 1\n  - 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n\nDiscretization:\n- n_point samples over [0,2], dx = 2/n_point\n- h[i] = 1 if x_i in interval 1 or 2, 0 otherwise\n- \u2211h[i] \u2248 n_point/2 due to interval total length 1\n\nInterval definition:\n- Interval i = [a_i, a_i + L_i], with a_0 and a_1, L_0 and L_1 fixed by total sum 1 and interval count (2)\n- No overlap between intervals: the intervals must be disjoint in [0,2]\n\nProjection:\n- Ensure feasible by adjusting interval boundaries if they fall outside [0,2]\n- Clip values to [0,1] if necessary\n\nObjective calculation:\n- C = max_k (dx * correlation(h, 1-h)[mode='full']).max()\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\nParent handling:\n- If parent initial_h_values is provided, consider both two-interval and old methods\n- For larger n_points, reduce iterations\n\nCore loop:\n- For trial in range(n_trials):\n  - Generate a and b (interval start points) uniformly distributed, then compute interval lengths\n  - Enforce disjointness and total measure 1\n  - Build dense vector h, compute C\n  - Update best solution if found\n\nRestart strategy:\n- Check best C periodically and reset if target not reached\n\nComparison with old:\n- Compare results across different n_points\n- Use sliding window to adjust positions\n\nSearch parameters:\n- n_trials = varying (large for main, smaller for restarts)\n- Start with n_point=400, then 500, then 600\n- max_overlap = 0.3808 target\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\n            Two-interval setup with length summation 1\n            Different interval separations\nretry:\n        Random search:\n        - Ensure intervals do not overlap\n        - Skip if overlap\n        - Different length distributions\n\n        Three-interval option (fallback if two-interval fails)\n        - For small n_points only\n        - Activated if two-interval doesn't improve\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\n        Time budget:\n        -n_trials * 15*N operations (N=vector length)\n        -Periodically check elapsed time\n        -Start with smaller n_trials for faster initial scan\n\n        Safeguards:\n        - Maintain validity of h (disjoint intervals, sum close to 1)\n        - Use proper reporting for checking\n        - Fallback to initial value if time runs out\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\n        x: returns best h, C, n_points = 400\n        o: ensures sum(h) \u2248 1/dx\n        o: h_bounds between 0 and 1\n        o: C computed independently to ensure precision\n\n        Initial algorithm: \n        o: Score based on C (lower = better)",
